            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Takes the natural logarithm of the sigmoid of every value, computed in one step so that it stays accurate where the sigmoid is tiny.\n\nThe sigmoid turns a score into a probability; logsigmoid turns it into a log probability. Doing it as two nodes (sigmoid then log) fails for very negative inputs, because the sigmoid rounds to exactly 0 and the log of 0 is minus infinity - logsigmoid of -100 is -100, which the two-step version cannot give you. Use it when you add up log probabilities, or want a curve that is flat at the top and straight at the bottom.\n\nTHE CURVE:\nAlways negative. Large positive inputs give values just below 0 (4 gives -0.018); 0 gives -0.693 (the log of one half); large negative inputs give almost the input itself (-4 gives -4.018). So it runs along the diagonal on the left and flattens onto zero on the right. It is the mirror image of softplus: logsigmoid of x equals minus softplus of minus x.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.nn.logsigmoid\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntensor in:\nThe tensor to bend. Receiving it triggers the node. A list, number or numpy array is turned into a float32 tensor on the cpu first; a tensor arriving as a tensor keeps its own dtype and device. The curve is applied to every element on its own, so the output has the same shape as the input. An INTEGER tensor (t.arange with whole numbers makes one) is refused: the console reports \"not implemented for 'Long'\" and nothing is sent. Put a t.to float32 in front of it.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\noutput:\nThe bent tensor, the same shape as the input.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nt.linspace makes 128 values running evenly from -4 (left edge of the plot) to 4 (right edge); the middle of the plot is 0. The plot draws two lines over each other: the straight diagonal is the input itself, and the other is the node's output for the same values. Where the two lines coincide the node passes the value unchanged; where they part, that is the bend. The plot's height runs from -4 to 4; the input line runs off the top and bottom where it leaves that range.\n\nRELATED:\nt.nn.sigmoid is the curve before the logarithm. t.nn.softplus is the same shape turned upside down and back to front. t.nn.log_softmax is the many-choice version: log probabilities across a whole vector.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 780,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 765,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "Takes the natural logarithm of the sigmoid of every value, computed in one step so that it stays accurate where the sigmoid is tiny.\n\nThe sigmoid turns a score into a probability; logsigmoid turns it into a log probability. Doing it as two nodes (sigmoid then log) fails for very negative inputs, because the sigmoid rounds to exactly 0 and the log of 0 is minus infinity - logsigmoid of -100 is -100, which the two-step version cannot give you. Use it when you add up log probabilities, or want a curve that is flat at the top and straight at the bottom.\n\nTHE CURVE:\nAlways negative. Large positive inputs give values just below 0 (4 gives -0.018); 0 gives -0.693 (the log of one half); large negative inputs give almost the input itself (-4 gives -4.018). So it runs along the diagonal on the left and flattens onto zero on the right. It is the mirror image of softplus: logsigmoid of x equals minus softplus of minus x.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.nn.logsigmoid\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntensor in:\nThe tensor to bend. Receiving it triggers the node. A list, number or numpy array is turned into a float32 tensor on the cpu first; a tensor arriving as a tensor keeps its own dtype and device. The curve is applied to every element on its own, so the output has the same shape as the input. An INTEGER tensor (t.arange with whole numbers makes one) is refused: the console reports \"not implemented for 'Long'\" and nothing is sent. Put a t.to float32 in front of it.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\noutput:\nThe bent tensor, the same shape as the input.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nt.linspace makes 128 values running evenly from -4 (left edge of the plot) to 4 (right edge); the middle of the plot is 0. The plot draws two lines over each other: the straight diagonal is the input itself, and the other is the node's output for the same values. Where the two lines coincide the node passes the value unchanged; where they part, that is the bend. The plot's height runs from -4 to 4; the input line runs off the top and bottom where it leaves that range.\n\nRELATED:\nt.nn.sigmoid is the curve before the logarithm. t.nn.softplus is the same shape turned upside down and back to front. t.nn.log_softmax is the many-choice version: log probabilities across a whole vector."
        },
